INT. MUNICIPAL SWIMMING BATH, SLOW LANE - EARLY MORNING

A Victorian bath hall, tiled the green of old bottles, steam lifting off the water in sheets. Iron galleries ring the pool. Behind the clerestory glass, a gray dawn.

COACH HESTER LINDQVIST-AMOAH (60s, tall, wool cardigan, stopwatch around her neck) stands at the pool's edge with her hands behind her back. She has watched ten thousand swimmers and no longer pretends to be surprised by any of them.

In the water, treading, is NINA OKONKWO-REYES (17, goggles pushed to her forehead, lips pale from cold), the best backstroker the club has had in a decade. Beside her, on the edge, sits her father, WILFREDO REYES (40s, work boots, a thermos, a face that has not slept).

A wall-mounted speaker coughs.

TOURNAMENT OFFICIAL (V.O.)
(over loudspeaker, distorted)
Heat four, lane three, you are called to the blocks. Heat four, lane three.

Nina does not move. Her father unscrews the thermos.

WILFREDO
That is you, mija.

NINA
I know it's me.

She pulls her goggles down. Pushes them up again. Pulls them down.

HESTER
Nina. Look at me.

She does. The coach crouches, joints cracking, and speaks in a voice that does not carry beyond the three of them.

HESTER (CONT'D)
You have trained every morning for six years. Whatever happens in the next minute and a half has nothing to do with that.

NINA
It has everything to do with that.

HESTER
Then you've nothing to fear.

NINA
You're not helping.

Hester almost smiles. She straightens, takes the stopwatch from around her neck, and presses it into the girl's wet palm. The girl looks at it, bewildered.

HESTER
Hold it. Don't start it.

TOURNAMENT OFFICIAL (V.O.)
(over loudspeaker)
Final call, lane three.

Nina's hand closes around the stopwatch. She hauls herself out of the water in one movement, streaming, steaming, and walks toward the starting blocks with her father's eyes on her back. He rises, half-lifts a hand, lets it fall. Hester touches his elbow.

HESTER (CONT'D)
She'll be fine.

WILFREDO
You said that last year.

HESTER
And last year she was.

He nods slowly. He looks down at the thermos in his hand as if wondering how it got there.

At the blocks, Nina bends. Her fingers curl around the edge of the starting platform. Her knees bend. Her shoulders fall.

The hall goes silent. Even the steam seems to hold.

TOURNAMENT OFFICIAL (V.O.)
(over loudspeaker)
Take your marks.

A beep. She is in the air.
